## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, the key finding is that the search results did not provide the specific cumulative number of confirmed COVID-19 cases for Georgia on September 14, 2020. Furthermore, the sources did not clarify whether the case and death figures for Georgia and North Carolina originated from the same official source using consistent methodology, nor did they confirm a consistent definition and reporting timeframe for the nursing home death comparison.

The information still lacking to finalize the fact-check includes the exact official case and death counts for both states on the specified date from a common source, and verified, comparable data on nursing home deaths for that period.

Given the absence of specific, methodologically consistent data from the recorded evidence to validate the numerical comparisons, the claim cannot be verified. Therefore, the knowledge from the fact-check does not support the claim, leading to the conclusion `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The fact-check refuted the claim because the available evidence did not provide the specific cumulative case and death counts for Georgia on September 14, 2020, nor did it confirm that the figures for both states [originated from the same official source using consistent methodology](https://www.nghd.org/covid-19-central). Furthermore, the sources reviewed did not establish that the nursing home death comparisons used a [consistent definition and reporting time frame](https://carolinapublicpress.org/30369/dhhs-reverses-stance-on-identifying-nursing-homes-with-outbreaks/), making the numerical claims unverifiable.